  "answer_text": "When speaking with someone who has a \"lazy eye\"\u2014which is medically known as **strabismus** (misaligned eyes, where one eye turns inward, outward, upward, or downward)\u2014it is natural to feel a bit unsure of where to look. You might worry about making them uncomfortable or feel self-conscious that your own eyes are darting back and forth. \n\nThe goal is to make the other person feel respected, comfortable, and heard. Use these highly effective techniques and etiquette tips to navigate eye contact naturally:\n\n### 1. Look at the \"Fixing\" (Dominant) Eye\nIn most cases of strabismus, one eye is actively focusing and looking straight at you (the \"fixing\" eye), while the other eye is drifted or misaligned. \n* **What to do:** Simply look at the eye that is looking directly at you. This will establish a clear, direct, and completely normal line of eye contact. \n\n### 2. Focus on the Bridge of Their Nose\nIf you are having trouble figuring out which eye is dominant, or if focusing on one eye feels too intense, use the \"bridge of the nose\" trick.\n* **What to do:** Focus your gaze on the spot right between their eyes or the bridge of their nose. \n* **Why it works:** To the other person, it looks exactly like you are making direct eye contact. More importantly, it keeps your own eyes stationary, preventing you from darting your gaze back and forth between their eyes, which can make them feel self-conscious.\n\n### 3. Soften Your Focus to the Whole Face\nIn normal human interaction, we rarely stare intently into a person\u2019s pupils for long periods. \n* **What to do:** Take a broader, \"soft\" focus of their face. Let your eyes naturally drift to their forehead, mouth, or general facial expressions as they speak. This makes the interaction feel much more relaxed and conversational.\n\n### 4. Practice Natural Communication Dynamics\nRemember that constant, unbroken eye contact can feel aggressive in any conversation. \n* **What to do:** It is completely natural to look away periodically\u2014especially when you are the one speaking, thinking of a word, or gesturing. Let your gaze drift naturally away and back to their face just as you would with anyone else.\n\n---\n\n### Crucial Social Etiquette Dos and Don'ts\n* **DO NOT look behind you:** If their drifted eye points over your shoulder, never turn around to see what they are looking at. People with strabismus are highly aware of where their eyes are pointing, and looking behind you can feel embarrassing or invalidating to them.\n* **DO NOT point it out:** Unless you are incredibly close friends or family and they bring it up first, do not ask, *\"Which eye should I look at?\"* or comment on their eyes. \n* **DO focus on the connection:** Ultimately, eye contact is just a tool for connection. Focus deeply on what they are saying, their tone of voice, and their ideas. When you focus on the person rather than their eyes, any awkwardness will naturally fade away.",
